ion {:value "256215"} "Kayak Singles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "256235"} "Kayak Doubles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "256241"} "Kayak Fours, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "256247"} "Canadian Singles, 500 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "256253"} "Canadian Singles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "256259"} "Canadian Doubles, 500 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "256265"} "Canadian Doubles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "256292"} "Kayak Singles, 500 metres, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "256298"} "Kayak Doubles, 500 metres, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "256304"} "Kayak Fours, 500 metres, Women"]] [:script {} "$('#same_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:select {:name "id", :id "other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "255468"} "1976"] "\n" [:option {:value "255607"} "1980"] "\n" [:option {:value "255701"} "1984"] "\n" [:option {:value "255809"} "1988"] "\n" [:option {:value "255953"} "1992"] "\n" [:option {:value "256092"} "1996"] "\n" [:option {:value "256337"} "2004"] "\n" [:option {:value "258763"} "2008"] "\n" [:option {:value "2008517"} "2024"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/162"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Kayak Doubles, 500 metres, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "27 September –  1 October 2000"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/753"} "Sydney International Regatta Centre, Penrith, New South Wales"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/256226"} "38 from 19 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Germany’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9894"} "Kay Bluhm"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9915"} "Torsten Gutsche"] " had won the men’s K-2 500 at the last two Olympics, but had both retired by the 2000 Games. Their nation had not earned a medal in this event at the European or World Championships since their 1996 victory, but sent the promising duo of " [:a {:href "/athletes/90189"} "Ronald Rauhe"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/90194"} "Tim Wieskötter"] " to Sydney. The 1996 Olympic bronze medalists, " [:a {:href "/athletes/8998"} "Danny Collins"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9041"} "Andrew Trim"] " of Australia, had been the 1997 World Champions and 1999 bronze medalists, while " [:a {:href "/athletes/90234"} "Marek Twardowski"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10537"} "Adam Wysocki"] " of Poland were the reigning European and World Champions. Slovakia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/90254"} "Juraj Bača"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/90258"} "Michal Riszdorfer"] " had been the 1998 World Champions, while " [:a {:href "/athletes/10159"} "Beniamino Bonomi"] " of Italy, one of the 1996 Olympic runners-up, had been the 1997 and 1998 World runner-up and the 1997 European champion with " [:a {:href "/athletes/10192"} "Luca Negri"] ", although he was partnered with double Olympic champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/10201"} "Antonio Rossi"] " in 2000."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Germany defeated Poland in the first opening heat to advance directly to the final, while Slovenia was bested by Hungary’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10072"} "Zoltán Kammerer"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/90204"} "Botond Storcz"] ", the latter of whom had been one of the most recent World runners-up. Australia and Italy, meanwhile, were sent to the semi-finals by Sweden’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10789"} "Henrik Nilsson"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10796"} "Markus Oscarsson"] ". Poland, Slovenia, and Italy met in the semi-final, which the latter won while Slovenia was eliminated in favor of Americans " [:a {:href "/athletes/11136"} "Peter Newton"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9382"} "Angel Pérez"] ". The final was a battle between Australia and Hungary, the latter of whom was able to push forward and capture the gold medal, leaving the former with silver at their home Olympics. The fight for bronze, meanwhile, was between Germany, France’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/90170"} "Bâbak Amir-Tahmasseb"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/9592"} "Philippe Aubertin"] ", and Poland, who finished in that order a mere 0.192 seconds apart. The Hungarians thus became double Olympic champions at these Games, as they had also been part of the crew that had won the K-4 1000 the day before."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/10072"} "Zoltán Kammerer"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/90204"} "Botond Storcz"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/HUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/HUN.png"}] "HUN"]] [:td {} "1:31.144 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "1:47.055 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/9041"} "Andrew Trim"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/8998"} "Danny Collins"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/AUS.png"}] "AUS"]] [:td {} "1:30.393 (2 h3)"] [:td {} "1:31.475 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "1:47.895 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/90189"} "Ronny Rauhe"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/90194"} "Tim Wieskötter"]] [:td {}